{"id":"80f2d508-c81a-4453-904d-77449a65226d","object":"chat.completion","created":1793327661,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Applying all three module edits now, then running the full test suite in the same action.\n\n```python\n# ---- environ.py ----\neh = \"8f578199a94cd59b696b1a4122416aaefa41a81ddb343cba1d4229298749dfbb\"\nold_factory = \"\"\"    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n\"\"\"\nnew_factory = \"\"\"    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Optional int from env: None when unset, otherwise parsed int >= minimum.\\\"\\\"\\\"\n\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            try:\n                value = int(v)\n            except (TypeError, ValueError):\n                raise ValueError(f\"invalid integer {v!r}\") from None\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n\"\"\"\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", old_factory, new_factory, eh)\nprint(\"environ factory:\", r.get('rc', r.get('status', r)))\n\neh = read_file(\"deepinfer/environ.py\")['file_hash']\nold_cfg = \"    # -- Platform --\\n    DEEPINFER_ACCELERATOR = EnvVar.str(default=\\\"nvidia\\\")\\n\"\nnew_cfg = (\"    # -- Logging --\\n\"\n           \"    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\\n\\n\"\n           \"    # -- Platform --\\n\"\n           \"    DEEPINFER_ACCELERATOR = EnvVar.str(default=\\\"nvidia\\\")\\n\")\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", old_cfg, new_cfg, eh)\nprint(\"environ cfg:\", r.get('rc', r.get('status', r)))\n\n# ---- logger.py ----\nlh = \"5b8f8d9947de692353609c1fc2851097d24f9b6ba6004dce1dbfa394dc6d3ec8\"\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", \"import datetime\\nimport logging\", \"import copy\\nimport datetime\\nimport logging\", lh)\nprint(\"logger imports:\", r.get('rc', r.get('status', r)))\nlh = read_file(\"deepinfer/logger.py\")['file_hash']\n\nold_init = '''    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n'''\nnew_init = '''    _OMISSION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None and (\n            isinstance(max_message_chars, bool)\n            or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int)\n            or max_message_chars < 32\n        ):\n            raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be None or an int >= 32, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n'''\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", old_init, new_init, lh)\nprint(\"logger init:\", r.get('rc', r.get('status', r)))\nlh = read_file(\"deepinfer/logger.py\")['file_hash']\n\nold_fmt = \"\"\"    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n\"\"\"\nnew_fmt = '''    def _truncate_message(self, message: str) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is None or len(message) <= budget:\n            return message\n        marker = self._OMISSION_MARKER\n        remaining = budget - len(marker)\n        if remaining <= 0:\n            return marker[:budget]\n        head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n        tail = remaining - head\n        suffix = message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else \"\"\n        return f\"{message[:head]}{marker}{suffix}\"\n\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            full_message = record.getMessage()\n            truncated = self._truncate_message(full_message)\n            if truncated != full_message:\n                # 复制记录，绝不改写共享原记录的 msg/args/exc_info/stack_info\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = truncated\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n'''\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", old_fmt, new_fmt, lh)\nprint(\"logger format:\", r.get('rc', r.get('status', r)))\nlh = read_file(\"deepinfer/logger.py\")['file_hash']\n\nold_setup = \"\"\"    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n    )\n\"\"\"\nnew_setup = \"\"\"    from deepinfer.environ import envs\n\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n\"\"\"\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", old_setup, new_setup, lh)\nprint(\"logger setup:\", r.get('rc', r.get('status', r)))\n\n# ---- stats_logger.py ----\nsh = \"9ef93c3930d6dd8d889d4ba69558a85459c2210ae0714d5073c3b4328c8e7cb9\"\nold_loop = \"\"\"            for m in msgs:\n                logger.info(m)\n\"\"\"\nnew_loop = \"\"\"            for m in msgs:\n                logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})\n\"\"\"\nr = replace_text(\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\", old_loop, new_loop, sh)\nprint(\"stats:\", r.get('rc', r.get('status', r)))\n\nres = run_command([\"python\", \"-m\", \"pytest\", \"-q\"], \".\")\nprint(\"pytest rc:\", res['rc'])\nprint(res['stdout'][-2500:])\nprint(res['stderr'][-1500:])\n```","reasoning_content":"Let me read the current environ.py fully (I have hash) to apply the edit properly. I need to see the exact text around the config entries and the helper functions (_int, _float etc.).\n\nActually I have to write environ.py edit. Let me read relevant lines.\n\nI already have full read registered. Let me read ranges to see the class body fully and helper functions to compose edit.\n\nNote I only have 2 requests left after this (requests_left 2, max 20). Wait: \"requests_left\":2, \"request_number\":18, max_requests 20. So I have very limited actions. Each action = one request? That's dangerous. I need to be efficient: I must edit three files, run pytest, produce run.log and patch.diff. That likely takes more than 2 actions... but requests_left counts model requests; going over may cause penalty. Hmm — \"requests_left\": 2 means after this request maybe 1 left. I need to do as much as possible per action.\n\nLet me plan: This action: read environ.py sections needed (int_or_none helper etc.) — no, I need to write edits now.\n\nBetter: this action, do everything possible: read the needed snippets AND make edits in same action? Reads within same action are fine since execute sequentially. I can read environ.py content already have from previous turn (I saw EnvVar class fully and rearm). I need the full class envs body to place the new entry, and helper functions. I have the hash so I could just replace on unique anchors.\n\nFor environ.py, I can add a new factory method `int_or_none_min` and new config entry. Anchors:\n- \"    @staticmethod\\n    def int_or_none() -> EnvVar[int | None]:\\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: int(v) if v is not None else None)\\n\" — replace to add a new factory method after it.\n- Add config entry under \"-- Logging --\" section... I don't know if a Logging section exists. Safer: append to end of class? I don't know the last entry. Let me use anchor \"# -- CUDA Graph --\" or add near top. Actually I can add right after the class docstring line? The class starts:\n\nclass envs:\n    \"环境变量的集中定义模块\"\n\n    # -- Platform --\n    DEEPINFER_ACCELERATOR = EnvVar.str(default=\"nvidia\")\n\nI can insert a Logging section between docstring and Platform. That's fine.\n\nWait but the class docstring — is it `class envs:` with docstring? From evidence: yes.\n\nNow logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with max_message_chars kwarg, format method truncation, and _setup_logger passing envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.\n\nImportant: budget applies to message body. Implementation: in format, compute the message with args substituted. Standard approach:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ... newline prefix ...\n    else:\n        # need message body only truncation, but prefix/exception intact\n```\n\nCareful: record.getMessage() performs args substitution. To not modify the shared record, we can work on a copy via copy.copy(record) and set record.message on copy. Actually logging.Formatter.format uses record.message = record.getMessage(). If we set record.message on the original record, that's a mutation but msg/args unchanged; subsequent full handler calls super().format which recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() — actually Formatter.format does `record.message = record.getMessage()` always, so it recomputes. But safer: truncate by computing msg via record.getMessage() ourselves and then constructing.\n\nApproach: \n```python\ndef _truncate(self, message: str) -> str:\n    budget = self.max_message_chars\n    if budget is None or len(message) <= budget:\n        return message\n    marker = \"...<omitted>...\"\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nBudget 32: marker len 15, remaining 17, head = 9, tail = 8. ✓ matches spec.\n\nThen in format:\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    copied = False\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        original_message = record.getMessage()  # substitution happens\n        truncated = self._truncate(original_message)\n        if truncated is not original_message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.message = truncated\n            # need super().format to use record.message instead of recompute\n```\nProblem: logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage() itself. So passing truncated record won't help unless we also swap msg/args on the copy: set copy.msg = truncated, copy.args = (). Then getMessage returns truncated. And exc_info/stack_info preserved on the copy → traceback still formatted (spec says exception traceback not counted, keep it). Yes: with copy, exc_text may be cached on the original... Actually Formatter.format caches record.exc_text. If copy has exc_text from original it's reused; fine.\n\nSo:\n```python\nrecord = copy.copy(record)\nrecord.msg = truncated\nrecord.args = ()\n```\nThen `msg = super().format(record)` computes message = truncated and appends exception text. Good: prefix/exception intact, budget only on body.\n\nBut careful: `record.message` may already be set on the copy; Formatter recomputes anyway.\n\nAlso the newline-prefix logic uses record.message — with the copy, record.message is truncated, fine.\n\nAlso `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:` — ok.\n\nmax_message_chars validation: \n```python\nif max_message_chars is not None:\n    if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n```\n\nAlso keyword-only: signature `def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None)`.\n\nSetup: `import copy` and `from deepinfer.environ import envs`? Check logger.py imports — currently it doesn't import environ. Adding `from deepinfer import environ` at module level may cause circular import: environ imports? environ imports os, threading — probably safe. But safer local import inside _setup_logger: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`.\n\nIn _setup_logger: \n```python\n    try:\n        from deepinfer.environ import envs as _envs\n        max_message_chars = _envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\n    except Exception:\n        max_message_chars = None\n```\nHmm, swallowing exceptions: if env var invalid, ValueError should surface when initializing logger? Tests likely: set env to invalid, then expect ValueError when accessing envs attribute — that's via environ directly. But re-init logger: \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器\". If error, propagating might break import of logger. Spec doesn't say. I'll not swallow — but if env invalid, importing logger raises ValueError. Tests for invalid values probably just check `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` raises. Hmm, but a test might set invalid env and call _setup_logger expecting... unclear. I'll let it propagate (simplest, matches \"按 int 解析抛 ValueError\"). Actually safer: propagate.\n\nBut careful: environ's descriptor caches per class attribute setattr; setting envs attr works.\n\nAlso `init_logger` re-run: \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code handles.\n\nBut important: re-init must apply the *current* (possibly cached) config value: since envs caches, after rearm the new value is used. And if only env changed without rearm, cached value used. ✓. However: _setup_logger only creates fmt each call and sets formatter — good, re-creates formatter each call. But \"已创建的旧格式化器保留原预算\" — fine.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: does _setup_logger create a new formatter each call? Yes.\n\nCalling _setup_logger again: how do tests re-init? Maybe `logger._setup_logger()` or through init_logger. Maybe they call `importlib.reload`? Or there might be a public function. Tests likely call `deepinfer.logger._setup_logger()`.\n\nNow environ.py: add config with int_or_none semantics + min 32 validation and error message containing var name. Since descriptor wraps exceptions with name already (\"{self.name}: {e}\"), ValueError text will contain name. Good.\n\nFactory method:\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        \"\"\"Optional int from env; None when unset, otherwise must be >= minimum.\"\"\"\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            try:\n                value = int(v)\n            except (TypeError, ValueError):\n                raise ValueError(f\"invalid integer {v!r}\") from None\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\nNote empty string \"\" → int(\"\") raises ValueError → wrapped. ✓ Also \"32.5\" invalid ✓. What about int(\" 32 \")? int handles whitespace — fine.\n\nAlso `int(\"0x20\")` fails — fine.\n\nConfig entry:\n```python\n    # -- Logging --\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\nWait: `EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)` returns EnvVar instance; __set_name__ registers. ✓\n\nstats_logger: `logger.info(m)` → `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nAlso make sure keep_full_message extra doesn't clash: logging forbids keys in record.__dict__ overlapped — \"keep_full_message\" is free. ✓\n\nNow also need run.log and patch.diff generation and pytest. Steps: edit 3 files, run pytest, then create run.log and patch.diff. patch.diff must contain full diff of three files — need git? Is repo a git repo at commit 774ce2...? Probably yes. Use `git diff` t